The bug is a units mismatch. JWT `exp` is in **seconds** since Unix epoch, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**. So `Date.now()` is always ~1000x larger than `exp`, making every token appear expired — or if you're comparing the wrong way, never expired.

**Broken:**
```js
if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {  // Date.now() ≈ 1_700_000_000_000
  throw new Error('Token expired'); //   payload.exp ≈ 1_700_000_000
}
```

This condition is always true, so either all tokens are rejected, or (if the logic is inverted) none are.

**Fix — divide `Date.now()` by 1000:**
```js
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > payload.exp) {
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
```

Or equivalently, use `Date.now() / 1000 > payload.exp` (no floor needed for the comparison).

**Better approach** — add a small clock skew buffer to tolerate minor time drift between servers:
```js
const CLOCK_SKEW_SECONDS = 30;
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > payload.exp + CLOCK_SKEW_SECONDS) {
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
```

